 ## 5. Discussion and Limitations
 
-R1 does not support a stronger V harm advantage under communication. Its negative interaction combines a private-revision advantage with a small shared-proposal disadvantage. On protected worlds, sharing reduces harm for V (8.929 to 7.500), N (12.071 to 7.214) and U (11.071 to 5.214); N improves more than V. U has the lowest shared harm. The interaction is therefore a relative difference in communication benefit, not evidence that communication increases V harm. Animal outcomes tie. Interface-dependent N recommendations prevent a clean acquired-policy interpretation. R2 yields a V advantage under sharing, alongside a similar private advantage; it does not reverse the interaction into communication amplification. The sign of the shared-condition contrast changes across interfaces. This supports reporting both procedures, not selecting the favorable one as confirmation. The general hypothesis remains unresolved. Conventional human-centered morality could consistently protect humans while permitting bounded animal use. Human psychological associations do not establish equivalent mechanisms in language models.
